WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:18.432
Back in the 1950s and early '60s,

00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:22.499
if they had full-blown respiratory distress syndrome,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.099
more than 90% would die.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.866
It occurs in its most severe form for prematurely born babies,

00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:38.832
when the lungs have not yet matured.

00:00:38.833 --> 00:00:42.699
As a child, I was born in Auburn, New York,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.799
a wonderful place for a child to grow up.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.032
So, at age eight or nine,

00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:54.966
I'd already thought I wanted to be a scientist.

00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:58.199
I was able to gather materials to make

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.666
a huge Tesla coil,

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:11.699
which was 15,000 volts, and a kilowatt of power.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.299
My parents...

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.899
mother, a proper Victorian lady born in 1880.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.832
Now, my father, an attorney in general practice,

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.432
was also an intellectual person.

00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:31.732
It was obvious to me that my parents were pleased

00:01:31.733 --> 00:01:34.299
when I brought home good grades.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.966
So, I graduated at the top of my class,

00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:40.499
and I only applied to one university.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.166
That was Cornell in Ithaca.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.766
I went there.

00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:49.632
And so, I did college and medical school

00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:57.666
in five years, five and a half years.

00:01:57.667 --> 00:02:01.132
The questions that I came...

00:02:01.133 --> 00:02:04.866
was assigned to study dealt with nerve gases:

00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:10.399
sarin, tabun, gases like that.

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.399
The effects of those agents

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.399
-- anticholinesterase agents --

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.632
on the lungs.

00:02:16.633 --> 00:02:19.566
So, I became a physiologist,

00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:22.066
but I became a lung physiologist,

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:25.299
in spite of being out of school for just a couple of years

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:29.932
and being woefully ignorant about it.

00:02:29.933 --> 00:02:34.266
But then a marvelous thing happened.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:38.266
The army chemical center had contracts, research contracts,

00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:40.966
with people at universities.

00:02:40.967 --> 00:02:44.032
I was assigned one of those for a contract

00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.799
at the Harvard School of Public Health,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.199
where they were doing lung research

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.199
related to artificial ventilation

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:02.299
and assistance for soldiers with respiratory problems.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.666
Jere Mead was studying lung mechanics.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:12.699
But then Ted Radford came to that department

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:15.732
to do research with Jere.

00:03:15.733 --> 00:03:19.832
And he... Ted took a very physicochemical approach

00:03:19.833 --> 00:03:23.099
to lung problems.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:30.199
He was curious about the problems of oxygen uptake

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:31.899
through the lungs,

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.766
basically, how much area is required in the lungs,

00:03:36.767 --> 00:03:40.666
between the air and the capillary blood,

00:03:40.667 --> 00:03:46.166
to provide an adequate pathway for uptake of oxygen?

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:51.332
It occurs by diffusion; it's not an active transport.

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:58.566
So the only way you can get enough transferred

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:02.899
fast enough to support metabolic processes,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.899
especially under exercise,

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.599
is to have a very large area in the lungs.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.699
That was a given.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:16.999
That was supported by morphometric measurements,

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.999
cutting up tissue into slices,

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.466
doing geometric measurements on them,

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:23.766
and using those to estimate area,

00:04:23.767 --> 00:04:27.699
which turned out to be huge.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:32.966
If you unrolled the area of

00:04:32.967 --> 00:04:34.832
the small air spaces in the lung,

00:04:34.833 --> 00:04:37.699
it would cover... for one adult,

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:40.899
it would cover practically a football field.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.166
Ted Radford didn't think you could

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:45.132
fold up a football field

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.999
and get it inside the chest of a human adult.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.499
And so he wanted to estimate

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.332
the area by a different method,

00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:56.732
not histologic/morphometric methods.

00:04:56.733 --> 00:05:02.166
And he took the physicochemical approach.

00:05:02.167 --> 00:05:04.466
And he did, really,

00:05:04.467 --> 00:05:07.699
a very clever kind of experiment.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.966
He said, I want to know the area

00:05:10.967 --> 00:05:12.866
of the surface of the lung.

00:05:12.867 --> 00:05:15.599
And so what property does that have

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.699
that would have enable me to estimate it?

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.399
And he knew about surface tension.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:27.732
It's equivalent to the surface free energy

00:05:27.733 --> 00:05:30.699
of a liquid surface.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.699
And so he thought,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:36.666
well, if I can guess what the surface tension is

00:05:36.667 --> 00:05:38.532
in the lungs,

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:43.432
then I can divide that...

00:05:43.433 --> 00:05:46.566
the energy of the surface

00:05:46.567 --> 00:05:49.299
in order to calculate the area.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:52.932
So, a non-morphometric way of doing it.

00:05:52.933 --> 00:05:55.666
But then he had to find out

00:05:55.667 --> 00:05:58.832
what the energy of the alveolar surfaces

00:05:58.833 --> 00:06:00.999
totaled up to be.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.066
And he did that by doing...

00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:09.399
making pressure-volume studies on the lungs.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:14.999
So then, if you knew or could assume a surface tension,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.432
you could take that simple equation

00:06:17.433 --> 00:06:21.499
and divide the energy by the surface tension,

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:23.532
and that would...

00:06:23.533 --> 00:06:25.299
you would come out with the area.

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.699
That's what he did,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:31.432
and he got a value only one-tenth

00:06:31.433 --> 00:06:36.866
of the histologic/morphometric measurements.

00:06:36.867 --> 00:06:41.566
I mean, a ten-fold discrepancy between the two methods,

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:44.799
and each method was supposed to be okay.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.299
Now, what's going on here.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.499
Well, I talked with Ted about it,

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:52.932
and asked him to explain what was going on,

00:06:52.933 --> 00:06:56.299
you know, what the method actually meant.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:03.466
And Ted sort of drew himself up to his full height

00:07:03.467 --> 00:07:05.266
as an electrical engineer

00:07:05.267 --> 00:07:12.166
and said, well, you wouldn't understand it.

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:15.466
Well, I'm usually a mild-mannered person,

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:17.466
but that got under my skin.

00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:23.199
And I thought -- clenching my teeth --

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.399
I will understand this, you know,

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.566
without your help.

00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:35.632
That big discrepancy, you know, bothered me.

00:07:35.633 --> 00:07:39.332
And so I tried to think, where are the errors in this?

00:07:39.333 --> 00:07:41.066
Are there errors of method?

00:07:41.067 --> 00:07:43.666
Are there error of thinking?

00:07:43.667 --> 00:07:46.832
Errors of concept?

00:07:46.833 --> 00:07:48.899
So, I thought, well,

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.399
he's using data and an equation,

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.966
but he's assuming a surface tension

00:07:54.967 --> 00:07:58.499
for the inside of the lung.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:01.932
What if that's wrong?

00:08:01.933 --> 00:08:04.066
So, I thought, well,

00:08:04.067 --> 00:08:07.166
we can turn the calculations backwards.

00:08:07.167 --> 00:08:11.999
And we can say we know what the energy is,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:13.799
the surface energy.

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:17.599
And we know what the area is.

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:21.032
So we can do the division the other way around

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.966
and calculate the surface tension in there,

00:08:23.967 --> 00:08:27.466
from the energy and the area.

00:08:27.467 --> 00:08:29.632
From the pressure-volume diagram,

00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:31.532
you get an energy,

00:08:31.533 --> 00:08:35.466
and so you could calculate, in steps,

00:08:35.467 --> 00:08:41.999
during the deflation curve in the pressure-volume curve,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.332
what the surface tension would be at that point,

00:08:45.333 --> 00:08:48.866
during change along volume

00:08:48.867 --> 00:08:52.799
from maximum to minimum.

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.432
And the result was astonishing.

00:08:57.433 --> 00:08:59.766
It was absolutely amazing.

00:08:59.767 --> 00:09:03.532
Because the surface tension at the full amount of volume

00:09:03.533 --> 00:09:08.966
was a value close to serum,

00:09:08.967 --> 00:09:14.099
to tissue juice, to blood.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.699
And... but it then fell down to a value at minimal lung volume

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:23.499
close to zero surface tension.

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:26.232
That's a remarkable thing.

00:09:26.233 --> 00:09:28.966
And I knew enough about...

00:09:28.967 --> 00:09:31.432
I'd studied enough about surface tension at that point

00:09:31.433 --> 00:09:38.066
to know that that's not just

00:09:38.067 --> 00:09:41.299
an ectoplasmic manifestation.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:45.199
There has to be a substance to do that.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.899
That takes us to the next step, you know?

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:50.599
Which is, there is a substance,

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.232
and it behaves like a surfactant,

00:09:54.233 --> 00:10:07.566
you know, a material that can lower the surface tension of water.

00:10:07.567 --> 00:10:10.899
When I got to studying the effects of surface tension

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:13.166
in the lung,

00:10:13.167 --> 00:10:16.566
there was a wealth of ignorance.

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:19.566
You know, the people I talked to,

00:10:19.567 --> 00:10:21.166
the physiologists I talked to,

00:10:21.167 --> 00:10:23.999
didn't really get it.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:26.232
And it was a hard slog,

00:10:26.233 --> 00:10:29.432
because as I went along and did some experiments

00:10:29.433 --> 00:10:31.299
I thought I got useful information.

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.999
But it didn't...

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:38.332
it didn't get much acceptance among my peers.

00:10:38.333 --> 00:10:42.532
So, you know,

00:10:42.533 --> 00:10:44.866
when we wrote up our initial findings

00:10:44.867 --> 00:10:46.832
and tried to send it to Science,

00:10:46.833 --> 00:10:49.199
they turned it down.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.266
When we were still at the...

00:10:52.267 --> 00:10:55.866
at the crude extract level.

00:10:55.867 --> 00:10:58.432
Well, you can...

00:10:58.433 --> 00:11:01.066
you can see why people at a prestigious magazine

00:11:01.067 --> 00:11:03.799
would say, well, this isn't good enough.

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:07.599
We need to know what it really is.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:10.999
So, trial and error,

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:16.366
taking available chemicals off the shelf

00:11:16.367 --> 00:11:19.099
and mixing them together

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.499
and trying to imitate the properties...

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:26.399
pretty crude by today's standards.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.266
But, now, we're talking half a century ago.

00:11:31.267 --> 00:11:34.499
You probably would be surprised to learn,

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:37.666
if you haven't already,

00:11:37.667 --> 00:11:41.466
that in those days chromatography

00:11:41.467 --> 00:11:43.832
was in its very infancy.

00:11:43.833 --> 00:11:46.432
And you had to work to get a lot of material

00:11:46.433 --> 00:11:48.466
and work to purify it,

00:11:48.467 --> 00:11:51.399
and then characterize it by methods

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:54.632
that were very laborious compared to

00:11:54.633 --> 00:11:56.366
what are available today.

00:11:56.367 --> 00:12:00.266
So, the beginnings went very slowly.

00:12:00.267 --> 00:12:06.166
But by using the developing chromatographic methods

00:12:06.167 --> 00:12:11.766
and comparing with synthetic compounds...

00:12:11.767 --> 00:12:16.566
melting points, you know,

00:12:16.567 --> 00:12:18.899
boiling points,

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.966
determinations of molecular weight,

00:12:23.967 --> 00:12:26.132
methods of that sort.

00:12:26.133 --> 00:12:30.099
Far removed from the tandem mass spec

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.599
that we have today,

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:35.499
and better methods than that even.

00:12:35.500 --> 00:12:40.366
So, the studies of composition went slowly.

00:12:40.367 --> 00:12:45.399
But it became evident that the material

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:49.466
was built like a... like a membrane,

00:12:49.467 --> 00:12:51.199
like a cell membrane,

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.699
you know, rich in phospholipids,

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:57.499
a sprinkling of neutral lipids, some cholesterol,

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:59.266
and some proteins.

00:12:59.267 --> 00:13:01.566
Now, in an... in an...

00:13:01.567 --> 00:13:04.366
a sort of normal kind of cell membrane

00:13:04.367 --> 00:13:10.832
there'd be roughly 50% protein

00:13:10.833 --> 00:13:13.666
and 50% lipids of various sorts.

00:13:13.667 --> 00:13:18.966
In this membrane-like material that we were getting out of the lung,

00:13:18.967 --> 00:13:21.199
it was only 8% protein,

00:13:21.200 --> 00:13:24.166
and the rest was predominantly phospholipid.

00:13:24.167 --> 00:13:28.432
And one in particular,

00:13:28.433 --> 00:13:33.632
dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine.

00:13:33.633 --> 00:13:38.132
And that made up about 60% of the...

00:13:38.133 --> 00:13:41.966
of the mass of the material.

00:13:41.967 --> 00:13:45.766
So, that was a good pointer,

00:13:45.767 --> 00:13:50.666
because a saturated, highly polar phospholipid

00:13:50.667 --> 00:13:56.066
is wonderful to make films at water surfaces.

00:13:56.067 --> 00:14:01.466
That was already known in another part of the community.

00:14:01.467 --> 00:14:03.332
So, that was a hint for us.

00:14:03.333 --> 00:14:06.132
And we began to see what we could do

00:14:06.133 --> 00:14:09.132
with that particular molecule,

00:14:09.133 --> 00:14:32.032
DPPC for short.

00:14:32.033 --> 00:14:39.599
She did that at Christmastime in 1957.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:42.832
And I showed her everything,

00:14:42.833 --> 00:14:45.832
you know, the balance and the results,

00:14:45.833 --> 00:14:48.332
and what ideas I had about it.

00:14:48.333 --> 00:14:49.932
And she went back to Boston,

00:14:49.933 --> 00:14:53.466
and she and Jere Mead

00:14:53.467 --> 00:14:56.766
set up a surface balance of their own

00:14:56.767 --> 00:15:00.799
and made extracts from the lungs of babies who had died,

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:05.532
and found -- or didn't find, I should say --

00:15:05.533 --> 00:15:08.566
surfactant in the lungs of those babies

00:15:08.567 --> 00:15:12.232
who had died with respiratory distress syndrome.

00:15:12.233 --> 00:15:15.499
But if the babies were mature enough

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:19.599
-- if they weighed more than about 1000 grams at birth --

00:15:19.600 --> 00:15:26.532
then they could find good surface activity.

00:15:26.533 --> 00:15:31.199
So, they got enough data to write a paper

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:33.299
and then publish it.

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:38.699
It came out in January of 1959.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:42.232
So, the next Christmas,

00:15:42.233 --> 00:15:46.966
I got my Christmas present from them.

00:15:46.967 --> 00:15:49.832
When that was published,

00:15:49.833 --> 00:15:54.666
some of the doubters began to think

00:15:54.667 --> 00:15:56.299
there might be something to it,

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:58.766
and many, many people around the world

00:15:58.767 --> 00:16:03.432
got interested and began devoting research time to it.

00:16:03.433 --> 00:16:07.566
There were lots of people trying to work on producing surfactants,

00:16:07.567 --> 00:16:09.166
some of them synthetic,

00:16:09.167 --> 00:16:12.199
some of them natural variants.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.166
I designed a synthetic.

00:16:14.167 --> 00:16:17.099
I didn't like the idea of some

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:19.766
animal-derived surfactants

00:16:19.767 --> 00:16:26.266
because of sensitivity and contamination considerations.

00:16:26.267 --> 00:16:30.499
So, I tried to design a completely synthetic one,

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:33.599
which I did,

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:36.599
and which the Burroughs Wellcome Company, as it was known back then,

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:38.466
licensed.

00:16:38.467 --> 00:16:41.066
It was patented by the University of California,

00:16:41.067 --> 00:16:43.999
and they licensed it and produced it in quantities

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:51.632
sufficient to do proper clinical trials.

00:16:51.633 --> 00:16:54.132
And the results were,

00:16:54.133 --> 00:16:56.266
again, very promising,

00:16:56.267 --> 00:17:00.866
but now based on much larger numbers of patients.

00:17:00.867 --> 00:17:06.932
By the time the FDA approved it,

00:17:06.933 --> 00:17:10.499
the number of patients in the study

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.099
really exceeded the number of patients

00:17:13.100 --> 00:17:17.999
in any other clinical study that had been done.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:22.566
So, the FDA was justified

00:17:22.567 --> 00:17:24.966
-- well, I think, you know --

00:17:24.967 --> 00:17:27.799
in approving it.

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:34.799
Now, taking all birth weights into account,

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:41.699
the mortality of RDS is less than 5%.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:44.132
We have very dramatic graphs.

00:17:44.133 --> 00:17:49.232
But we have to qualify that

00:17:49.233 --> 00:17:54.366
by saying that care in the newborn intensive care unit

00:17:54.367 --> 00:17:58.932
has advanced tremendously since the early days.

00:17:58.933 --> 00:18:03.399
Neonatology as a specialty has come into being.

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:07.032
It didn't even exist at the beginning.

00:18:07.033 --> 00:18:10.932
And one has to give proper credit.

00:18:10.933 --> 00:18:15.499
Well, a surfactant addition to that armamentarium

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:17.932
is a relatively recent one,

00:18:17.933 --> 00:18:21.566
really since 1990.

00:18:21.567 --> 00:18:27.166
So, that's 40 years after the beginning of the field.

00:18:27.167 --> 00:18:34.632
And from then until 2000,

00:18:34.633 --> 00:18:41.866
the mortality curve just went practically to 0.

00:18:41.867 --> 00:18:44.632
I still get the occasional letter

00:18:44.633 --> 00:18:47.232
from a proud mother

00:18:47.233 --> 00:18:54.299
who thanks me for my contributions to the field.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:19:18.000
You know, that's very satisfying.

